    {"role": "assistant", "text": "This is a classic scale-out problem. At 500K RPS, you're well beyond what a single Python process can handle, especially with ML inference. Here's how I'd approach the rearchitecture:\n\n**Phase 1: Decouple inference from serving**\nSplit the model serving into a dedicated inference tier. Move the scikit-learn model into a dedicated service using something like Ray Serve, Triton Inference Server, or even a simple FastAPI service with proper worker management. The key insight is that model loading (often 1-10 GB) should happen once at startup, not per request.\n\n**Phase 2: Add a caching layer**\nRecommendation systems often have high temporal locality — the same user or item appears in many requests within seconds. Redis with a TTL of 30-60 seconds can absorb 60-70% of requests before they hit the model.\n\n**Phase 3: Horizontal scaling**\nDeploy multiple inference replicas behind a load balancer. Your 500K RPS distributed across 50 replicas is 10K RPS per replica — very manageable.\n\n**Phase 4: Async processing**\nFor non-real-time recommendations (email, push notifications), switch to async batch processing via Kafka or SQS.\n\nWhat's the current latency requirement? Is this synchronous user-facing or can it tolerate 100-200ms?", "index": 1},
