    {"role": "assistant", "text": "Sub-50ms p99 with a two-tower model at scale — this is very achievable but requires a specific architecture. The key insight is that you don't need to run the full model at query time.\n\n**The Approximate Nearest Neighbor (ANN) pattern:**\n\nPre-compute all item embeddings offline (nightly or hourly batch job). At query time, you only need to:\n1. Compute the user embedding (single forward pass through the user tower)\n2. ANN search against pre-indexed item embeddings\n\nThis reduces query-time compute by 80-90%.\n\n**Recommended stack:**\n- **User tower inference**: ONNX Runtime or TensorFlow Serving, GPU-backed, ~5-10ms\n- **ANN search**: FAISS (Facebook AI Similarity Search) with IVF+PQ index, ~2-5ms for 10M items\n- **Result assembly + filtering**: Redis for item metadata, ~1-2ms\n- **Total**: ~10-20ms model time + network overhead\n\n**FAISS index type for your scale:**\n```\nFlatL2: Exact but O(N) — too slow at 10M+ items\nIVF4096,Flat: Good balance, ~5ms for 10M items\nIVF4096,PQ32: Compressed, ~2ms but slight accuracy loss\n```\n\nFor 50M users with fresh recommendations, you'll also want user embedding caching — compute and cache user embeddings every 15 minutes rather than per-request.\n\nWhat's your item catalog size? And how often does it change?", "index": 3},
