    {"role": "assistant", "text": "Index hot-swapping without downtime is one of the trickier operational challenges. Here's the pattern that works at production scale:\n\n**Blue-Green Index Deployment:**\n\n```python\n# Pseudocode for hot-swap\nclass FAISSIndexManager:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.active_index = 'blue'  # or 'green'\n        self.indexes = {'blue': None, 'green': None}\n        self.locks = {'blue': RWLock(), 'green': RWLock()}\n    \n    def swap(self, new_index_path):\n        inactive = 'green' if self.active_index == 'blue' else 'blue'\n        # Load into inactive slot (no readers yet)\n        with self.locks[inactive].write():\n            self.indexes[inactive] = faiss.read_index(new_index_path)\n        # Atomic pointer swap\n        self.active_index = inactive\n```\n\n**Incremental update strategy for 30-min cycles:**\n\n1. **Delta index**: New items in last 30min go into a small FAISS index (~50K items)\n2. **Main index**: Full catalog rebuilt weekly during low traffic\n3. **Query time**: Search both delta and main, merge top-K results\n\nThis keeps delta index small (fast to rebuild every 30min) while main index is only rebuilt weekly.\n\n**For removed items:**\nDon't delete from FAISS (expensive). Instead, maintain a Redis set of 'tombstoned' item IDs and filter them from results post-search. Compact the main index during weekly rebuild.\n\n**Capacity planning:**\n5M items × 128-dim float32 = 2.56GB for flat embeddings. With IVF+PQ32 compression: ~320MB. Very manageable.\n\nThe weekly model retraining means you'll need to recompute all 5M item embeddings. At ~50K items/sec on a GPU, that's 100 seconds — easily done overnight.", "index": 5},
