    {"role": "assistant", "text": "You've hit one of the fundamental challenges in recommendation systems: the offline-online metric gap. AUC-ROC is measuring the wrong thing for recommendations.\n\n**Why AUC-ROC fails:**\nAUC measures ranking ability on historical data. But recommendations are about future behavior on unseen items. The model may 'overfit' to popular items in historical data, showing great AUC but poor online diversity/novelty.\n\n**Better offline metrics:**\n\n1. **NDCG@K** (Normalized Discounted Cumulative Gain): Measures ranking quality with position discounting. More aligned with real recommendation positions.\n\n2. **Hit Rate@K**: Did the user interact with any of the top-K recommendations? Simple but highly correlated with online CTR.\n\n3. **Catalog Coverage**: What % of items appear in top-10 recommendations across users? Low coverage indicates popularity bias.\n\n4. **Diversity (Intra-List Similarity)**: Average pairwise cosine distance between recommended items. High similarity = boring recommendations.\n\n5. **Serendipity**: Are recommendations surprising yet relevant? Hard to measure offline but important for engagement.\n\n**The right evaluation protocol:**\n```python\n# Time-based split (not random!)\ntrain_data = events before 2024-01-01\nval_data = events 2024-01-01 to 2024-01-07  # Hold-out week\n\n# Evaluate on 'next interaction' prediction\n# For each user, hide their last 5 interactions, predict them\nfor user in val_users:\n    known_items = user.history[:-5]\n    target_items = user.history[-5:]\n    predictions = model.recommend(user, known_items, k=50)\n    metrics.update(hit_rate(predictions, target_items))\n```\n\nRandom splits leak future information into training — always use time-based splits.\n\n**The gate for auto-promotion:**\n- Hit Rate@10 improvement ≥ 0.5% vs current production\n- Catalog Coverage ≥ 95% of previous model\n- No regression in latency p99\n- If gate passes: shadow deploy for 1h, then 1% traffic, then full rollout", "index": 11},
