    {"role": "assistant", "text": "A production recommendation system needs three layers of monitoring. Missing any one layer means you'll be flying blind during incidents.\n\n**Layer 1: Infrastructure Metrics (Prometheus + Grafana)**\n\n```python\n# Key metrics to instrument\nREQUEST_LATENCY = Histogram('inference_latency_seconds',\n    buckets=[.005, .01, .025, .05, .1, .25])\nMODEL_INFERENCE_TIME = Histogram('model_inference_ms')\nFAISS_SEARCH_TIME = Histogram('faiss_search_ms')\nCACHE_HIT_RATE = Counter('cache_hits_total', ['tier'])\nGPU_UTILIZATION = Gauge('gpu_utilization_percent')\nINDEX_STALENESS = Gauge('faiss_index_age_seconds')\n```\n\nAlerts:\n- p99 latency > 40ms → PagerDuty (approaching 50ms SLA)\n- Cache hit rate < 70% → investigate traffic pattern shift\n- GPU utilization > 90% → auto-scale trigger\n- FAISS index age > 35 minutes → index update job failed\n\n**Layer 2: ML Model Health Metrics**\n\n```python\n# Embedding drift detection\ndef monitor_embedding_distribution(embeddings_batch):\n    mean_norm = np.linalg.norm(embeddings_batch, axis=1).mean()\n    variance = np.var(embeddings_batch, axis=0).mean()\n    \n    # Alert if distribution shifts significantly\n    if abs(mean_norm - baseline_norm) / baseline_norm > 0.05:\n        alert('embedding_drift_detected')\n```\n\nKey ML metrics:\n- **Prediction score distribution**: Track mean/std of recommendation scores. Sudden shift = model issue\n- **Item diversity**: Average pairwise distance in recommendations\n- **Coverage**: % of catalog appearing in recommendations\n- **Freshness**: % of recommended items added in last 7 days\n\n**Layer 3: Business Metrics (real-time)**\n\nStream to ClickHouse or BigQuery for real-time analysis:\n- Click-through rate per position (position bias detection)\n- Conversion rate (recommendation → purchase)\n- Revenue attribution from recommendations\n- User satisfaction proxy: skip rate (skipped item after seeing = implicit negative)\n\n**Unified Alerting Runbook:**\n```\nP0 (immediate page): p99 > 45ms OR error rate > 1%\nP1 (30min response): p99 > 35ms sustained OR cache hit < 60%\nP2 (next business day): CTR drops > 5% vs 7-day average\n```\n\n**Anomaly detection for silent failures:**\nThe sneakiest bugs return valid responses with wrong recommendations. Add statistical process control — track metric percentiles and alert on 3-sigma deviations from rolling 7-day baseline.", "index": 17},
    {"role": "human", "text": "Very comprehensive. Now let's discuss the feature engineering in more detail. For the user tower, what features actually matter most? We have access to purchase history, browse history, search queries, demographic data (age/gender/location), and device type.", "index": 18},
    {"role": "assistant", "text": "Feature importance varies significantly by dataset, but based on research and production experience, here's the empirical ranking for recommendation systems:\n\n**Tier 1 — High Signal (always include):**\n\n1. **Purchase history (sequence)**: The strongest signal. Use a transformer-based sequence encoder (BERT4Rec style) rather than simple aggregation. The order and recency of purchases matters enormously.\n   ```python\n   # Recent 50 purchases as token sequence\n   user_sequence = [item_id_1, item_id_2, ..., item_id_50]  # chronological\n   user_emb = bert4rec_encoder(user_sequence)  # [128]\n   ```\n\n2. **Browse history (last 200 items, time-weighted)**: Strong for capturing current session intent. Weight recent items 3-5x more than items from > 7 days ago.\n\n3. **Search queries (last 20)**: Explicitly signals user intent. Encode query text with a pre-trained sentence encoder (MiniLM works well) and aggregate.\n\n**Tier 2 — Moderate Signal (include if available):**\n\n4. **Category affinities**: Aggregated from history. What % of purchases/views are in each category? Robust signal that changes slowly.\n\n5. **Price sensitivity**: Average purchased price vs average browsed price. Users who browse $500 items but buy $50 items need different recommendations than those who buy what they browse.\n\n6. **Device type**: Mobile users want visually striking items, desktop users browse more systematically. Small but consistent signal.\n\n**Tier 3 — Weak/Noisy Signal (use with caution):**\n\n7. **Demographics (age/gender)**: Directionally useful but legally complex (fairness concerns). Many recommendation systems deliberately exclude these for bias reasons.\n\n8. **Location**: Useful for locally-relevant items, near-zero value for non-local products.\n\n**The temporal decay function:**\n```python\ndef time_weighted_history(events, half_life_days=7):\n    weights = []\n    for event in events:\n        age_days = (now - event.timestamp).days\n        weight = 0.5 ** (age_days / half_life_days)  # exponential decay\n        weights.append(weight)\n    return np.average([e.embedding for e in events], weights=weights)\n```\n\n**Important: Feature cardinality management**\nItem IDs in history need embedding tables. With 5M items, the embedding table is 5M × 32 = 640MB per dimension. Use frequency-based truncation: embed top 1M items, bucket the rest as 'other_item'.", "index": 19},
